  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Fri Jul 17 2026\n\nCurrently the large upper level ridge continues over the \nwestern United States. This ridge is carrying upper level \nMonsoon moisture from Arizona, through the northern Rockies, and\nover through the Dakotas. This moisture has brought in elevated\nshowers into southwest North Dakota this morning, and they now \ncontinue in the south central. Along with these showers is a \nlarge area of mid level clouds across the southern half of the \nstate. These clouds are prohibiting anymore diurnal heating, and\nmost of the south is stuck in the upper 70s. This area will \nlikely not hit the NBM high of the lower 90s.\n\nThis weekend the very hot ridge will continue, leading to high\ntemperatures back in the lower 100s by Sunday. Saturday night a\nvery very slight shortwave could form off the Montana mountains\nand bring some showers to the state. The NBM chances have \nreally backed off on them this morning, and that is nicely \nreflected in the global models as well. Sunday the ridge axis \nmoves directly over North Dakota, bringing the warmest \ntemperatures of the weekend in. Expects highs along and west of \nthe Missouri River to be in the lower 100s. We will likely need \na Heat Advisory for Sunday. Elsewhere will be in the lower to \nmid 90s. Sunday evening and night a weak cold front will sweep \nthrough, leading to a very breezy Monday. From this, the north \non Monday will be cooler in the upper 70s and lower 80s, while \nthe south will stay in the 90s. Since the state has had very \nlittle rain and very hot temperatures, the grasses have started \nto brown. With the potential of 40 mile per hour northwest \nwinds, the brown grass, and low humidities, we have started to \nmessage near-critical fire weather concerns across the \nsouthwest on Monday. \n\nFor the rest of next week, the warming trend will restart after\nTuesday. Tuesday the upper level pattern turns to the northwest,\nleading to the coolest day of the week. Expect highs in the mid\n70s north to lower 80s south. The rest of the week the ridge\nbuilds back in, starting the warming trend all over again.\nTemperatures could return to the upper 90s by next week. The NBM\npercentile ranges are starting to hint at that by next Friday.",
